# The application: the gateway. Each role's initramfs provides its own binary
# under this one name, so PID 1 needs no configuration and there is nothing to
# mis-set.
#
# Started once the clock is synchronised (see `timesync`), and stopped if the
# keeper ends. When it ends — cleanly or not — the guest ends with it (see
# `boot`).
#
# It runs as a user of its own, `app` (1000, image/initramfs), handed what it
# opens by `app-files`. As root it would own whatever root does, capabilities
# or not — the kernel's settings, PID 1's files — and `lockdown` keeping those
# read-only is then the second line, not the only one. It holds what
# `capabilities` raises with `^` (dinit keeps capabilities across the change of
# user, then raises these into the ambient set), and nothing in the image
# carries file capabilities to add to it; not being root, an exec gives it
# nothing more, and it has nothing to become root again with. The gateway
# holds one: `cap_sys_resource`, to lift the hard limit on its descriptors to
# what its largest tuning could need (`reserve_descriptors`,
# crates/gateway/src/main.rs).
#
# Its stdin, stdout and stderr are the console, as a process PID 1 starts has
# always had here: `console=null` on a production command line discards them,
# and the `debug` one puts a real console on the serial port. What may reach the
# host goes to the log device by name (crates/safe-logger), never through these.
type = process
command = /bin/app
depends-on: timesync
restart = false
run-as = app
capabilities = ^cap_sys_resource
options: shares-console
